Draw the two boundary lines from their intercepts. The line \(x + y = 50\) joins \((50, 0)\) to \((0, 50)\), and the line \(2x + y = 80\) joins \((40, 0)\) to \((0, 80)\). With \(x, y \ge 0\), both inequalities are of "less than or equal" type, so the feasible region is the part of the first quadrant lying below both lines.
Along the x axis the tighter restriction is \(2x + y \le 80\), giving \(x \le 40\); along the y axis it is \(x + y \le 50\), giving \(y \le 50\). The two lines cross where \(x + y = 50\) and \(2x + y = 80\); subtracting the first from the second gives \(x = 30\), and then \(y = 20\).
So the feasible region is the bounded quadrilateral with corner points \(O(0,0)\), \(A(0,50)\), \(B(30,20)\) and \(C(40,0)\). Since it is bounded, the maximum of Z occurs at one of these corners. Evaluating \(Z = 10500x + 9000y\):
at \(O(0,0)\), \(Z = 0\);
at \(A(0,50)\), \(Z = 9000 \times 50 = 450000\);
at \(B(30,20)\), \(Z = 10500 \times 30 + 9000 \times 20 = 315000 + 180000 = 495000\);
at \(C(40,0)\), \(Z = 10500 \times 40 = 420000\).
The greatest of these is \(495000\). Hence Z is maximum at \(x = 30\), \(y = 20\), with maximum value \(Z = 495000\).
